nt fatal incident in  the United States that we know of is  about 149 Americans dying from fake  heparin which is a blood thinning drug  but often it's very difficult to know if  you've got a condition like leukemia oh  you've got a heart condition if you have  a heart attack  no one's likely look for the did the  drug not work you know the evidence is  usually swallowed so I would argue that  there are more cases than are generally  reported but it is a lower problem in  the u.s. what is the government's role  in  of regulating this or controlling the  flow of prescription drugs and what what  are the kind of market approaches okay  well the FDA Food and Drug  Administration does a reasonable job of  overseeing the production plants where  they can get to them and so that  regulation I think is important in and  of itself but also because it encourages  better behavior it's a kind of carrot  and stick patients can't possibly tell  the difference between a good pill and a  bad bill so it's it's important that  there's regulation there it's enforced  but of course the FDA can't have  unfettered access to production plants  in China they have to give notice now  I'm a typical guy my wife if I've oh no  she's coming home in an hour and  apartments a mess I can tidy it up in an  hour if you're given a weeks notice in a  Chinese production plant you can  probably make it look pretty good so the  FDA has a role but given how much we get  from overseas 80% of the ingredients in  our drugs come from overseas it comes  down to the companies primarily everyone  in the distribution chain has a role to  play in monitoring for problems the most  recent problem was fake Avastin which is  a cancer drug and it was found by the  distributor as new technologies  developer new markets develop are we  seeing all of the factors in the supply  chain getting better at monitoring  things so is this a problem that will go  down or is it a problem that is starting  to get out of control the 